soc/tegra: fuse: read the platform from HIDREV on TF-A based SoCs

Since 8b8ee2e56f95 ("soc/tegra: Use ARM SMCCC to get chip ID, revision, and
platform info", Linux 7.2) the platform comes from bits [15:8] of the SMCCC
SoC revision. The TF-A firmware on Tegra194 and Tegra234 puts the HIDREV
major revision there ((major << 8) | minor, 0x401 on Orin), so the kernel
reports "Asim QT" and tegra_is_silicon() returns false; nvgpu then runs
about 7x slower. Read the platform from HIDREV again on Tegra210, Tegra186,
Tegra194 and Tegra234, and keep SMCCC for Tegra241 and later. Chip ID and
revision still come from SMCCC.

Tested on Linux 7.3 on an Orin NX 16 GB and two AGX Orin 64 GB.
Upstream status: not submitted.

--- a/drivers/soc/tegra/fuse/tegra-apbmisc.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/tegra/fuse/tegra-apbmisc.c
@@ -78,15 +78,35 @@
 		return TEGRA_SMCCC_MINOR_REV(revision);
 #endif
 	return (tegra_read_chipid() >> 16) & 0xf;
+
+}
+
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_HAVE_ARM_SMCCC_DISCOVERY)
+/*
+ * The TF-A firmware on these SoCs implements the SMCCC SoC ID call, but it
+ * reports the major revision in bits [15:8] of the SoC revision rather than
+ * the platform. Their platform has to be read from HIDREV instead.
+ */
+static bool tegra_smccc_has_platform(void)
+{
+	switch (tegra_get_chip_id()) {
+	case TEGRA210:
+	case TEGRA186:
+	case TEGRA194:
+	case TEGRA234:
+		return false;
+	}
 
+	return true;
 }
+#endif
 
 u8 tegra_get_platform(void)
 {
 #if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_HAVE_ARM_SMCCC_DISCOVERY)
 	s32 revision = arm_smccc_get_soc_id_revision();
 
-	if (revision >= 0)
+	if (revision >= 0 && tegra_smccc_has_platform())
 		return TEGRA_SMCCC_PLATFORM(revision);
 #endif
 	return (tegra_read_chipid() >> 20) & 0xf;
